<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Swedish Crusade</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1249</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Swedish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a> was a military expedition traditionally dated to 1249, in which a Swedish force led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birger-jarl">Birger Jarl</a> subdued the Tavastians of inland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>. Known mainly from the 14th-century Erik's Chronicle, it brought Tavastia under Swedish rule and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hame-castle">Häme Castle</a> was built to hold the region. Historians still debate its date and character.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-swedish-crusade.jpg" alt="Second Swedish Crusade" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph Alanen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Alanen_-_Conquest_of_H%C3%A4me.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> had held a foothold in Finland Proper since the First Swedish Crusade. Swedish missionaries, possibly led by Bishop Thomas, were active in Tavastia around 1230, and the Tavastian uprising of 1236–1237 gave the Swedes a reason to invade, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ix">Pope Gregory IX</a> calling on them to attack the Tavastians. Although Erik's Chronicle presents the expedition as a war of conquest, historians tend to see it as an unusually bloody phase in the longer incorporation of Finland into the Swedish state, in which Finnish chieftains who joined the Swedes gained power and prestige.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The chronicle gives no year, placing the expedition only before King Erik's death in 1250. In the 16th century Mikael Agricola dated it to 1248, and Paulus Juusten gave 1249, which became the traditional date. In the 19th century Peter Andreas Munch argued from the Saga of Haakon Haakonarson, written in 1264–1265, that a Swedish-Norwegian crisis kept Birger near the Norwegian border until the summer of 1249.</p><p>In 1946 Jarl Gallén held that the chronicle's chronology could not be trusted and proposed 1238–39, after the papal call and before the Swedish defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a>; Dick Harrison also finds the early date most probable. Rolf Pipping in 1926 and Igor Pavlovich Shaskol'skii defended the later dating, and Seppo Suvanto and Mauno Jokipii noted that Birger became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarl">jarl</a> only in 1248.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In August 1249 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> under his special protection without mentioning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. Bero, who came from the Swedish court, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in 1248 or 1249, and in 1249 the first monastery in Finland, a Dominican convent, was founded beside the bishop's fortification at Koroinen. From 1249 sources generally treat Finland Proper and Tavastia as part of Sweden, and the diocese first appears among the Swedish dioceses in 1253.</p><p>According to the chronicle the expedition cost Birger the crown: he was away when King Erik died in 1250, and the lords led by Joar Blå chose his young son Valdemar as king. Few records of the following decades survive, as Novgorodian forces burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turku">Turku</a> in 1318. The last Swedish crusade to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, against the Karelians, came in 1293.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Swedish Crusade” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Swedish expedition led by Birger Jarl, known as the <b>Second Swedish Crusade</b> and traditionally dated 1249, subdued Tavastia and built Häme Castle.</blockquote><a href="/state/tavastia">Tavastia</a></li></ul></section>
